41 "field-1" 13194139534718 true] [17592186305275 1104 #edb/ref 17592186305276 13194139534718 true] [17592186305275 1110 #uuid "a23e7946-284a-55e7-bd2a-78859a2edc8e" 13194139534718 true] [17592186305276 1034 "12" 13194139534718 true] [17592186305276 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534718 true] [17592186305276 1111 #uuid "f0e34fd9-fd35-5d6d-ac9c-036a6bfa9ec1" 13194139534718 true] [17592186305277 1017 #edb/ref 17592186305278 13194139534718 true] [17592186305277 1089 "q-343474" 13194139534718 true] [17592186305277 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534718 true] [17592186305277 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040051/assets/29c0f5b8a290434e8c20f652beb73b245192c6e122f16a28f45b2029bde53a6d.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $y=f(x),$ shown above. It consists of five line segments. Find the value of\n${\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534718 true] [17592186305277 1113 #uuid "3b74f787-c5b2-5002-863a-4c1ca936dbcd" 13194139534718 true] [17592186305277 1145 "The graph consists of $2$ parts: the three line segments for $x\\in [-5,0]$ and the two line segments for $x\\in [0,5].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,0].$\nThe corresponding region is a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+5)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,5].$\nThe corresponding region is also a trapezoid, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(a+b)h \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2+5)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2} \\\\  & =7-7 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534718 true] [17592186305278 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305279 13194139534718 true] [17592186305278 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534718 true] [17592186305278 1041 "field-1" 13194139534718 true] [17592186305278 1104 #edb/ref 17592186305279 13194139534718 true] [17592186305278 1110 #uuid "f7933472-4d64-5051-9c10-1fdeb69a977a" 13194139534718 true] [17592186305279 1034 "0" 13194139534718 true] [17592186305279 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534718 true] [17592186305279 1111 #uuid "9b08716d-f1e2-568f-9667-74aea991ded0" 13194139534718 true] [17592186305280 1017 #edb/ref 17592186305281 13194139534718 true] [17592186305280 1089 "q-343963" 13194139534718 true] [17592186305280 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534718 true] [17592186305280 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040051/assets/a1c6da313c90821bfaa6e4fe178bcca79032ea8c1b97838b83c1b85cc70ef9ec.png)\n\nConsider the graph of the function $g(x),$ shown above. It consists of two line segments and an arc of a semi-circle centered at $(-1,3)$ with radius $1.$ Find the value of  ${\\int }_{-4}^{3}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n${\\int }_{-4}^{3}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534718 true] [17592186305280 1113 #uuid "9436756c-f918-50b1-b359-ec1a88236f1c" 13194139534718 true] [17592186305280 1145 "The graph consists of $3$ parts: the line segment for $x\\in [-4,-2],$ an arc of a semi-circle for $x\\in [-2,0],$ and another line segment for $x\\in [0,3].$ Therefore, we can write the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{3}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-4}^{-2}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-2}^{0}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{0}^{3}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}\n$$\n\nThe $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-4,-2].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}bh \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-2,0].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle with the semicircle (of radius $1$) cut out on the top, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =lw-\\frac{1}{2}\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3-\\frac{1}{2}\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =6-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [0,3].$ The corresponding region is a rectangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & =lw \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-4}^{3}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}+{A}_{2}+{A}_{3} \\\\  & =18-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534718 true] [17592186305281 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305282 13194139534718 true] [17592186305281 1000 #edb/ref 17592186305283 13194139534718 true] [17592186305281 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534718 true] [17592186305281 1041 "field-1" 13194139534718 true] [17592186305281 1104 #edb/ref 17592186305282 13194139534718 true] [17592186305281 1110 #uuid "f9aa6ecc-7648-58da-af95-5a8e057c66ac" 13194139534718 true] [17592186305282 1034 "18-\\frac{\\pi}{2}" 13194139534718 true] [17592186305282 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534718 true] [17592186305282 1111 #uuid "0d5e4f52-f989-560e-8ffd-c8020643ec0c" 13194139534718 true] [17592186305283 1034 "18" 13194139534718 true] [17592186305283 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534718 true] [17592186305283 1111 #uuid "33f96674-d